# Development workflow in the current conversation

Follow the [built-in workflow and optional project supplements](references/optional-project-context.md)
for entry, unavailable Server data, project supplements, and Verifier
acceptance. That contract governs current behavior when historical guides
describe a mandatory online connection. The built-in method and actual checks
continue locally.

The current Codex conversation authors documents and code. `formsy-sop flow`
records stages and execution evidence, and FormSy CLI owns native capture and
synchronization. The release uses seven stages in order: project principles,
requirements, technical planning, task breakdown, execution preview,
implementation, and acceptance. Acceptance includes checks, repair, and
retesting in the same stage. Bind check results to current code and retain
failure history and repair budgets. The requirements stage retains the
`specify` ID. Update a single specification through clarification, then review
it together. See [the requirements phase](references/requirements-phase.md).
Resume old flows using their saved stages. Reading a flow preserves its saved
profile. Confirmation, upload, and Verifier checks stay within their owning
operations.

Retained `versioned-v1` runs keep their exact adopted principle pin and task
applicability document. Never replace that basis with today's author document.
If an incompatible runtime invalidated unchanged inputs, use the installed
`flow recover-inputs PROJECT --request FILE` entry with `operation_id`, `run_id`,
`expected_revision`, saved `input_digests` and the actual recovery `reason`.
The native operation validates the retained inputs, execution snapshot,
candidate and original check evidence, then resumes the saved stages without
replaying them. It preserves invalidation/failure history and repair budgets;
recovery does not approve or complete acceptance. Changed inputs or evidence
continue through ordinary review or affected checks. Never edit run state.
When the same installation changed only managed method inventories and the
pinned engine version, provide `installation_before` with absolute
`project_config` and `runtime_config` paths to the installer's original bytes.
The engine verifies them against the frozen hashes, permits only that exact
installer delta and proves the original whole-workspace check fingerprint.
It retains the original hashes and records an explicit compatibility proof;
application, assertion and execution-setting changes remain ineligible.

## Read and resume efficiently

At the start of semantic work, including a stage change or new work within the
same stage, describe the next work in a
small JSON request containing `goal`, `expected_output`, and `nature` (nonempty
text, at most 2048 characters each). Pass it to the existing runtime prefix with
`flow context PROJECT --view agent --current-work FILE`. For example, drafting
requirements aims to produce a specification; clarification aims to resolve
specific ambiguities and produce questions or confirmed boundaries; semantic
self-check aims to compare the current draft with its obligations and produce
findings. Use the actual goal and materials. Keep these facts stable through
file reads and structural checks in the same work. Reuse the same request when
retrieving context during that work. Change the facts for substantive
work, not for each tool invocation. The first `task_context` in normal command
output carries the supplied facts and the saved request, plan and current-task
facts. It adds no routing labels, stage approval or checkpoint analysis. The
option projects this invocation only; it does not save another workflow state.

Use the actual paths and SHA-256 values in `flow context.method_reading` to
locate the current stage's resources. Reuse already read sections while path
and content version match. After a context refresh, stage transition, or
conversation compaction, read only missing, truncated, or changed sections
needed for the next operation. A directory listing establishes availability.

Keep the paths, content digests, and sections already read in handoff or
compaction summaries. Keep the current official documents, preparation, draft
and receipt paths, pending `condition_id` and `item_id`, and the next action.
On resume, inspect existing drafts and receipts before preparing a check.
Reading records track reading. Record semantic checks and approval through
their owning operations.

Save the current stage draft before checking its applicable obligations. Read
native rule text as needed and defer rules that become due in later stages.
Review actual cross-module dependencies and any execution, delivery, or
closure claims introduced by the material. Rule, material, scope, and dependency
changes invalidate affected evidence under the existing reuse contract.

## Start or continue

When the independent acceptance Stop hook is installed, actual
`flow enter/start/select` binds the current host session to the exact
project and run. Read-only browsing leaves binding unchanged. To attach an
existing task, use `flow stop-bind PROJECT --run-id RUN_ID` with the verified
session and run. The Stop gate follows the saved stage. For features it covers
`implement`, `finish`, and `reverify`. For Bugs it covers `fix` and `test`.
Principles, requirements, clarification, planning, task breakdown, preview,
and Bug assessment can pause for input or review, including when revisited.

Once in an execution or acceptance stage, Stop reads the saved acceptance
state and its validity. Missing, stale, or unreadable completion requires
continuation. Read the saved state even when `stop_hook_active` is set. Explicit
refresh and formal acceptance observe changes to sources, materials, and
receipts. Stop itself only reads the saved observation. A saved result awaiting
review can return to the user with that status. The user can interrupt at any
time. Preserve bindings, recorded state, and check requirements. Verify
installation, exact-definition trust, and a real host callback separately
using the packaged SOP installation guide.

Read [the stage contract](references/workflow.md) and follow the actual stage in
`flow context`. Technical planning, task breakdown and consistency analysis,
local impact preview, and self-check are built in. Run them when due and keep
drafts separate from executed receipts. Read Birdview, project SOP, knowledge,
and Verifier as optional project supplements under the current stage and
configuration. Record missing configuration, authorization, or reads as
specific gaps and complete the built-in local flow.

When the user is testing, rehearsing, or accepting FormSy itself, treat the
workflow product as the deliverable and its sample requirement as test input.
Keep the sample run and interpret continuation against the test objective.
Record defects against the workflow product. Verify the distributed Skill,
CLI, and MCP, and repair missing behavior at the actual entry point.

For authorized development, identify the real repository and scope. The CLI
prepares local resources and attempts online project matching. Verify each
candidate separately using [repository candidates and Server matching](references/repository-candidates.md).
Record online availability gaps. Save the original request to a real local file,
then run `formsy-sop flow enter PROJECT --feature specs/FEATURE --request-file FILE --worktree TARGET`.
Use `--title TITLE` when a title is needed. Reuse the run for the same feature and
request. For a separate request when a current run exists, verify the old run
and use `enter --switch-from OLD_RUN_ID`. Resume a paused request with
`flow select PROJECT --run-id RUN_ID --worktree TARGET` using its original ID. Entry stores the
request, checks the project binding, and reads the initial architecture
context. Entry creates or resumes the native Task and associates the actual
execution worktree. PROJECT retains workflow materials; TARGET is the existing
worktree used for this operation. For manual entry of
an existing target, use `flow start`. For continuation, use
`flow context PROJECT --json`.

Use the explicit feature, paths, `current_stage`, `revision`, input digests,
and instructions in that context. Ordinary questions, explanations, and
read-only browsing stay outside `enter/start`. The current conversation owns
editing authority.

The default is guided review. Complete one step at a time and show its
deliverables and action card. At `awaiting_review`, present artifacts and
check results, then ask for the decision with the Codex
`request_user_input_async` dialog. The workbench provides reading and status.
Bind the question to the actual project, run, stage, revision, input digests,
and artifact versions. Keep the real question ID and answer source. A shown
dialog, default option, timeout, dismissal, or deferral leaves approval
pending. For read-only requests or a request to hold the stage, keep the
stage and its decision pending. A
`revise` needs actual user feedback on the current version. If the native
question tool is unavailable, state the gap and ask in the conversation.
See [presentation](references/presentation.md).

After `flow interact` accepts actual confirmation, continue in the same
conversation through the new current stage and stop at its next
`awaiting_review`. Keep actual stage reviews and reuse the user's existing
execution authorization. Select automatic mode only when the user explicitly
requests automatic continuation. User approval comes from the user's actual
decision. Read the [common contract](references/共同约定.md) and the
[workflow operation contract](references/workflow.md).

## Execute through the current conversation

Read the target project's `.formsy-sop/runtime.json`. When configured, append
full CLI arguments to its exact `cli_argv` array. Use argument arrays and
keep host paths local. The packaged `scripts/run_flow.py` reads the setting
and delegates. If it is absent, use the project's `tools/formsy-flow.py` or
the installed `formsy-sop` on PATH. The suffix
`flow context PROJECT --json` is the same for each. Report actual dependency
or configuration errors and leave PATH changes to explicit setup.

1. For new work, call `flow enter PROJECT --feature specs/FEATURE --request-file FILE --worktree TARGET`.
   Keep request identity and feature stable so replay selects the existing run.
   To continue, call `flow context PROJECT --json`. For the same target in another
   worktree or a retained run, use `flow select PROJECT --run-id RUN --worktree TARGET`.
   Keep PROJECT's workflow materials and the original Task; prepare a missing TARGET
   before entry and verify the returned receipt before operating there.
2. Follow the current stage's returned `prompt` and read the applicable product,
   technical, and self-check methods. When available at `constitution`, use
   `flow shared-refresh PROJECT` for the actual bound architecture supplement.
   When configured, `flow context` attempts project SOP and current-stage
   knowledge reads. Missing project setup, binding, or read authorization
   leaves those sources unchecked. Use `flow knowledge-refresh PROJECT` for
   an explicit retry.
3. Write the real documents or code in the current conversation. Keep the
   initial `revision` and `input_digests` and list the completed outputs.
   Submit a result only when the work it describes exists.
4. Save the result JSON to
   `.formsy-sop/runs/<actual-run-id>/requests/<stable-operation-key>.json`
   and call `flow advance PROJECT --result PATH`. See the
   [workflow operation contract](references/workflow.md) for fields and
   Preview execution parameters. Verify the engine receipt before reporting
   advancement. For stale versions, refresh and inspect actual changes.
5. At `finish/reverify`, verify retained check receipts against the current
   target. Run `flow check PROJECT --operation-id KEY --expected-revision N`
   for missing, failed, stale, or affected checks. Keep valid results when
   entering `reverify`. Read exit codes and logs. On failure, repair the actual
   appended tasks, retry with a new operation key, and retain failure history.
   After completing the review, submit `findings:[]` as the current model
   review conclusion. Independent audit and actual execution have their own
   evidence.
6. After each successful operation, read fresh context. The Kit starts the
   independent local Web workbench; open its URL in the right browser panel
   and let it poll the saved artifacts, following
   [presentation and interaction](references/presentation.md). Report an
   unavailable local view as a gap. In guided mode, show the latest deliverables
   and checks at `awaiting_review`, ask the version-bound native question, and
   wait for an actual response before `interact`. Once real confirmation is
   accepted, follow the receipt's continuation using fresh `revision` and
   `input_digests` in the same conversation. Knowledge refresh can increment
   revision. Preserve run, original confirmation operation, and designated
   next stage. Stop at its `awaiting_review`. Replay, a pending review, and a
   completed final stage without an eligible continuation preserve existing
   work and approvals. Guided mode leaves adoption, publication, and upload
   to their own authorization. Use the user's conversation language for
   user-facing text and controls, retaining necessary terms.

For a legacy workbench operation, verify actual
`project_directory/worktree/run_id/expected_revision/input_digests`. The
visible action and request link identify the intent. Full arguments stay
in the backend request file. Use the target as `--request-ref` and the
visible action as `--request-action`. For `confirm`, `revise`, `rerun`, or
`guided`, call `flow interact PROJECT --request-ref REF --request-action ACTION`.
For other actions, first call
`flow context PROJECT --request-ref REF --request-action ACTION --json`
to validate and read `workbench_request` internally, then use the existing
operation. Knowledge reads may change revision. Keep the original run and
action and use the latest context version for stage work. Require original
byte SHA-256, exact project, current run, request directory, operation, and
matching action and version. Reject mismatches. Keep internal instructions,
JSON, and digests out of visible replies. Refresh a legacy card without a
digest before using its request. `continue` runs the current step, `refresh`
updates context and display, and a user declaration on an atomic document
item records that item's status. Stage confirmation follows the stage
decision contract.

For an older saved page request, verify the actual
`worktree/run_id/revision/input_digests` and call
`flow interact PROJECT --request FILE` for `confirm`, `revise`, `rerun`,
or `guided`. Retain the actual user action. Refresh stale cards, reuse
`operation_id` for exact replay, and preserve materials, history, and
repair budgets during revise or rerun. Ordinary questions leave stage
execution idle.

`flow check` holds the project lock during checks. Read-only
`flow context` and workbench show, status, and detail can read a
versioned snapshot during execution without refreshing supplements or
advancing. Check commands must stay outside same-project writer operations
such as render, advance, or check while the lock is held. Use `--version`,
`flow --help`, or an independent read-only validator for an entry point
check. Configure actual business checks for the project. Navigation is
read-only. Verify real context before executing a workbench continuation.

Follow [Server project data](references/server-project-data.md) for
knowledge and Verifier. Recall experience during requirements and planning,
carry the original Verifier only when the corresponding experience is
adopted, and show the association in Preview. Read its original definition
and run it during acceptance or reverification. Preserve original responses,
applicability, execution results, and history separately. Review the
adopted scope instead of selecting an independent verification set.

## Stage work

Use the coordinator's fixed outputs. Principles go in `constitution.md`,
requirements and clarification in one `spec.md`, technical design in
`plan.md`, and task breakdown and consistency in `tasks.md` and
`analysis.md`. Keep technical research, data, and interface decisions in
`plan.md`. Use the existing scope, impact, and check views for Preview.
Implementation delivers code. Acceptance delivers actual check and repair
results. Keep internal preview JSON and process notes in their owning
workflow records.

List official stage outputs only in `flow advance.outputs`. Use temporary
directories for outlines, command responses, query snapshots, review drafts,
and coverage calculations. Store runtime requests, check receipts, and
recovery state under `.formsy-sop/runs/<run>/`. Put conclusions in the
corresponding document. At cleanup, remove only unreferenced working drafts
and preserve records that support current review or execution.

After drafting or substantially changing principles, requirements, or
technical documents, compare applicable atomic conditions to current
evidence, save the result, and recheck it according to
[presentation and interaction](references/presentation.md). Inspect
`context.document_selfcheck` for gaps. Check when each document is written.
Use formal receipts for those checks. If Server rules are unavailable,
complete the common document checks and report project and full-method
coverage gaps separately.

During domain screening, use the [numeric precision rule](references/numeric-precision-domain.md)
only for applicable tasks. Keep its domain card linked to the principles or
scope decision, requirements, plan, tasks, and the original checks carried
from Preview through Acceptance.

- **`constitution`**. Use the original Spec Kit constitution template,
  user requirements, and existing README and docs to generate project principles
  when the declared file is missing or still a template. Reuse existing principles
  for ordinary tasks; revise them only when the user requests a principle change.
  Keep task-specific scope and checks in the feature documents. Read available
  principles in later stages; missing principles or adoption records do not block
  specification. Complete the local document when Birdview is missing and record
  the shared-data gap. Active required material from the actual project binding
  supplements those principles. Read the pinned published Birdview architecture and
  rules catalog and record publication independently of adoption.
  `stage_recall` at this stage reads native project principles and the
  published Birdview rule catalog. Task-relevant business experiences,
  their bodies, and their Verifiers become relevant in later stages.
  Separately configured active required context follows its existing read
  contract. Keep the published-rule count separate from experience recall.
  Report experience recall as inapplicable at this stage.
- **`specify` (legacy `clarify`)**. Follow
  [the requirements phase](references/requirements-phase.md) and update a
  single `spec.md` through clarification. Read the product fields, template,
  PC rules, and sources. Use the [product mapping](references/spec-product-mapping.md).
  Recall relevant project experience using the actual requirements.
  Record selections and reasons for conflicts and exclusions. Keep one
  specification and one requirements state. Cover boundaries and acceptance,
  clarify material unknowns individually, and state when none block progress.
  Save the first draft and revise it as answers arrive.
- **`plan`**. Use technical methods for modules, interfaces, data, scope, and
  real checks. Recall experience relevant to the technical questions.
  Locate active rules in the actual knowledge entries and `source_refs`.
  Keep Birdview proposals identified as proposals. Outline work areas and
  leave executable task checkboxes to `tasks_analyze`.
- **`tasks_analyze`**. Write executable checkbox tasks with dependencies and
  acceptance conditions in `tasks.md`. Check consistency in `analysis.md`.
  When available, read the complete project supplement snapshot and record
  adopted items. Always save built-in atomic document self-check receipts
  covering requirements, design, tasks, and principles. Record semantic
  judgments as model judgments, and obtain runtime evidence separately.
- **`preview`**. Carry forward existing requirement acceptance items and the
  original Verifiers of adopted experience. Show source, stage, version, and
  association. Read exact Verifier definitions during acceptance. Guidance
  without a Verifier remains guidance. Follow
  [acceptance scope](references/acceptance-scope.md) for the retained list.
  Save `preview.json` with actual material versions, adopted rules, planned
  checks, and unresolved points. Use `flow preview-local` to record
  source-backed local predicted impact. When available, add the pinned
  Birdview baseline and diagram comparison. Identify predictions and gaps,
  and use separate authorization for publication.
- **`implement`**. After the engine saves execution context, complete the
  current tasks within the authorized scope. Preserve requirements, plan,
  original task definitions, and existing acceptance assertions. Mark task
  progress with checkboxes and record each batch with `flow progress`.
  Update the workbench using the [operation contract](references/workflow.md).
  Continue remaining tasks and isolate blocked dependencies. Submit this
  step after its tasks are complete.
- **`finish` (legacy `reverify`)**. Review pending, failed, unknown,
  missing-evidence, and actually affected items using existing findings,
  repair tasks, requirement acceptance, and adopted original Verifiers.
  At first acceptance, cover all requirements without current evidence.
  Preserve valid unaffected evidence and failure history. Changed targets
  or assertions need revalidation. Explicit audits retain their own
  obligations. Run actual checks through `flow check`. The program appends
  repair tasks from supported failures. Repair within budget in the current
  conversation and retest. Completion validates native execution evidence
  mechanically and records `acceptance_basis`. Do not require a model verdict
  or complete inline logs to confirm commands, targets, exit codes or hashes.
  Keep optional semantic advice and caller-reviewed manual observations
  separate from program facts; neither replaces missing or failed checks.
  Program-only requirements with exact frozen commands use native check
  results directly. Do not require an additional caller or model pass.

For knowledge governance or aggregate documents, read the corresponding
knowledge or assemble module. They are built-in SOP methods. Server project
knowledge is a stage-specific supplement. TraceAudit is an independent
candidate review with its own evidence.

Use the knowledge phase `understand` for `constitution/specify/clarify`,
`plan` for `plan/tasks_analyze/preview`, `execute` for `implement`, and
`verify` for `finish/reverify`. The first read in a stage caches the
result, including failures. Use an explicit retry to read again. Retain the
actual response snapshot, digest, read scope, and `updated_at`. Use stable
versions for identity. Re-review affected materials when adopted required
content changes in ways that affect this run. Completed-run browsing
preserves the original completion evidence and avoids new supplemental reads.

## Identity and capture

A Session is the actual capture session. A Task is a native deliverable
within it. Native lifecycle receipts attribute activities. The workflow
run organizes stages, documents, and review. Verify context and actual
trigger references under the Kit capture contract. Workflow enter/select creates
or resumes the native Task and associates its execution worktree. Reuse the Task
for the same target across worktrees. Stage transitions and `tasks.md` checkboxes
do not create native Tasks. Project knowledge and rules use project-level
read authorization. Retain historical business bindings and failure
receipts as history and use current authorization for new actions.

## Record and hand off

Record key outcomes as `handoff`, decisions as `decision`, actual reviews
or checks as `verification`, and blockers as `blocked`. Ordinary
conversation and navigation use the native event log. Once designated
artifacts are saved, follow the existing authorized capture and sync
contract. If native state or the bridge is missing, report that files are
saved and archiving is pending. After interruption, inspect actual side
effects before replaying an operation.

## Unified workbench

For a new task, follow [presentation and interaction](references/presentation.md)
to preserve questions, decisions, document versions, and self-check detail.
Record the current change in each reply and save actual answers before
continuing long work. Keep question tracking separate from the stage
summary. Built-in templates and modules provide methods. The general
profile uses original common SOP review methods and the actual machine
subset. The server profile uses rules actually adopted from independently
read project sources. Keep their sources, versions, receipts, and gaps
separate. Full PC, TC, KB, TASK, security, quality, and domain review each
require applicable evidence.

## Independent Bugs

Use `flow enter --profile bugfix` for an independent Bug and follow the
[three-stage Bug contract](references/bugfix-workflow.md) and pinned Spec
Kit instructions for `assess`, `fix`, and `test`. Retain the three
reports, actual original reproduction, current regression evidence, and
adopted original Verifiers. Repair inside feature development uses the
existing run. Existing runs keep their saved profile.

When the user requests merging the older completion and reverification
stages, and neither has started, use
`flow merge-acceptance PROJECT --input REQUEST.json`. Include the actual
`run_id`, `expected_revision`, original `input_digests`, a stable
`operation_id`, and the user's original reason. Preserve history, checks,
failure budgets, and the current stage and decision. Keep the original
sequence when acceptance has already started.
